    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for the fourth term of the sequence \\(3^0, 3^0 + 3^1, 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2, \\ldots\\), we start by identifying the general form of the sequence.\n\nThe first term of the sequence is:\n\\[3^0 = 1\\]\n\nThe second term is:\n\\[3^0 + 3^1 = 1 + 3 = 4\\]\n\nThe third term is:\n\\[3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 = 1 + 3 + 9 = 13\\]\n\nThe fourth term is:\n\\[3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + 3^3 = 1 + 3 + 9 + 27 = 40\\]\n\nTo verify the pattern, we can express each term in the sequence as the sum of a geometric series. The \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence is the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of the geometric series \\(1, 3, 9, \\ldots, 3^{n-1}\\).\n\nThe sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of a geometric series with the first term \\(a = 1\\) and common ratio \\(r = 3\\) is given by:\n\\[S_n = \\frac{a(r^n - 1)}{r - 1} = \\frac{3^n - 1}{3 - 1} = \\frac{3^n - 1}{2}\\]\n\nFor the fourth term (\\(n = 4\\)), we have:\n\\[S_4 = \\frac{3^4 - 1}{2} = \\frac{81 - 1}{2} = \\frac{80}{2} = 40\\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified value of the fourth term of the sequence is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{40}\n\\]",
